\[
A = \begin{pmatrix} 
-\frac{2}{3} & -\frac{2}{3} & -\frac{1}{3} \\ 
-\frac{2}{3} & \frac{1}{3} & \frac{2}{3} \\ 
-\frac{1}{3} & \frac{2}{3} & -\frac{2}{3} 
\end{pmatrix}
\]
The eigenvalue equation is $Av = v$, which simplifies to $(A - I)v = 0$, where $I$ is the identity matrix. So we need to find the null space of the matrix $A - I$. The matrix $A - I$ is:
\[
A - I = \begin{pmatrix} 
-\frac{5}{3} & -\frac{2}{3} & -\frac{1}{3} \\ 
-\frac{2}{3} & -\frac{2}{3} & \frac{2}{3} \\ 
-\frac{1}{3} & \frac{2}{3} & -\frac{5}{3} 
\end{pmatrix}
\]
We row reduce this matrix to find the eigenvector. The row reduction process gives us:
\[
\begin{pmatrix} 
1 & 0 & -1 \\ 
0 & 1 & -1 \\ 
0 & 0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix}
\]
This tells us that the eigenvector is of the form $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$. Dividing by the greatest common divisor of its components, we get the direction vector $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$. </think> <answer> $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} $ </answer>

